---
/**
 * /guides/warrior-2nd-job - Warrior 2nd job branches guide.
 *
 * SEO target: 'maplestory warrior 2nd job classic', 'MSCW fighter
 * vs page vs spearman', 'maplestory classic sword or axe warrior',
 * 'v83 warrior 2nd job build'. Returning-player intent focused on
 * the 30->70 progression window.
 *
 * Verification posture: skill LISTS are canonical v83 (well-documented
 * across hidden-street, mapleroyals wiki, sources cited in
 * skill-changes.astro). 2nd-job kits have NOT been datamine-verified
 * for CoT 2 yet (osmsdataexplorer.com is 1st-job focused). Numeric
 * values are v83-baseline pending live verification during
 * Founder's Access. This page inherits skill-changes.astro's
 * verification stance.
 */

import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
---

<BaseLayout
	title="Warrior 2nd Job Branches: Fighter, Page, Spearman - Liven's Classic Maple Guide"
	description="You hit Level 30 and the Warrior instructor asks you to pick a branch: Fighter (Sword/Axe), Page (Sword/Blunt Weapon), or Spearman (Spear/Polearm). Full skill kits, build orders, weapon speed tradeoffs, and party-role identity for all three, framed against canonical v83 baseline."
	currentPath="/guides"
	theme="perion"
	decorations={false}
>
	<section class="section-heading">
		<div>
			<span class="eyebrow">2nd Job Branches</span>
			<h1>Warrior: Fighter, Page, or Spearman?</h1>
			<p class="lede">
				At Level 30 you talk to the Warrior instructor in Perion and get
				the branching quest. This is the biggest identity-defining
				decision your Warrior makes: your entire weapon lineup, party
				role, and 3rd-job path all lock the moment you accept. Here's
				the full breakdown of all three branches, their skill kits,
				build orders, and who wins at what.
			</p>
		</div>
	</section>

	<article class="prose">
		<div class="callout callout--framing">
			<strong>Verification:</strong> skill LISTS below are canonical
			v83 (broadly cross-referenced across hidden-street /
			mapleroyals docs, and consistent with the CoT 2 first-job kits
			datamine-verified in
			<a href="/guides/skill-changes">Skill Changes Per Class</a>).
			2nd-job kits have NOT been datamine-verified for CoT 2 yet -
			skills and values assume v83 baseline until Founder's Access
			(October 6) lets us confirm live. Nexon moved Power Knockback
			from Bowman 2nd job to Bowman 1st job, so shuffles at this tier
			are demonstrably possible.
		</div>

		<h2 id="summary">The three branches at a glance</h2>
		<div class="guide-summary-grid">
			<div class="guide-summary-card guide-summary-card--changed">
				<h3>Fighter <span class="status-pill status-pill--new">DPS</span></h3>
				<p>
					<strong>Sword or Axe.</strong> The pure damage branch.
					<em>Rage</em> is a party-wide weapon-attack buff that shows
					up in every attacker's numbers - Fighters make good party
					members even before their own damage matters. Becomes
					Crusader at 70, Hero at 120.
				</p>
			</div>
			<div class="guide-summary-card guide-summary-card--changed">
				<h3>Page <span class="status-pill status-pill--rework">TANK/DEBUFF</span></h3>
				<p>
					<strong>Sword or Blunt Weapon.</strong> The oddball branch
					- lower damage than Fighter, but <em>Threaten</em> lowers
					enemy damage and <em>Power Guard</em> reflects hits.
					Historically the least-picked; genuinely shines against
					high-attack bosses. Becomes White Knight at 70, Paladin at 120.
				</p>
			</div>
			<div class="guide-summary-card guide-summary-card--changed">
				<h3>Spearman <span class="status-pill status-pill--new">SUPPORT</span></h3>
				<p>
					<strong>Spear or Polearm.</strong> The party-support
					branch. <em>Hyper Body</em> multiplies party max HP/MP -
					single most desired buff in the game post-70. Damage is
					competitive with Fighter; Polearm gets AoE swings.
					Becomes Dragon Knight at 70, Dark Knight at 120.
				</p>
			</div>
		</div>

		<h2 id="the-decision">The decision, in one paragraph</h2>
		<p>
			If you want the highest personal damage and don't want to think
			about it: <strong>Fighter</strong>. If you want to be the party's
			buff-bot and don't mind lower solo damage until Hyper Body:
			<strong>Spearman</strong>. If you specifically love tanking
			bosses and want a rarer identity: <strong>Page</strong>. Every
			branch is viable at endgame - none is "wrong" - but Spearman is
			the most-invited to parties because Hyper Body is that good.
		</p>

		<hr />

		<h2 id="fighter">Branch 1: Fighter</h2>
		<p class="section-lede">
			The Sword-or-Axe branch. Pure attacker identity. If you played
			any physical class in any MMO ever, this feels like home.
		</p>

		<h3>Weapon choice: Sword vs Axe</h3>
		<p>
			Both branches of Fighter (Sword and Axe) share the same skills
			with weapon-specific masteries and boosters. The real difference
			is <strong>attack speed vs base damage</strong>:
		</p>
		<ul>
			<li><strong>Sword</strong>: faster (Normal-4 speed on 1-handed,
				Normal-5 on 2-handed). Smoother DPS, more Final Attack
				proc chances per fight, better feel for grinding.</li>
			<li><strong>Axe</strong>: slower (Slow-5 on 1-handed, Slow-6
				on 2-handed) but higher base weapon damage and higher
				mastery damage variance ceiling. Bigger crit swings.</li>
		</ul>
		<p>
			<strong>Sword is the safe pick.</strong> Axe has flashier numbers
			but the slower speed makes it harder to weave with Final Attack
			procs, and 1-handed Sword + Shield (or Cape) is the "classic
			Fighter" silhouette everyone recognizes. Pick Axe if you
			specifically want the slow-crit playstyle.
		</p>

		<h3>Fighter skill kit (v83 baseline, 7 skills)</h3>
		<div class="skill-list-wrap">
			<table class="skill-list">
				<thead>
					<tr>
						<th>Skill</th>
						<th>Type</th>
						<th>Max Lv</th>
						<th>What it does</th>
					</tr>
				</thead>
				<tbody>
					<tr>
						<td><strong>Sword Mastery</strong></td>
						<td>Passive</td>
						<td>20</td>
						<td>+Sword mastery %, +accuracy. Damage floor gets closer to damage ceiling. Max this if you're going Sword.</td>
					</tr>
					<tr>
						<td><strong>Axe Mastery</strong></td>
						<td>Passive</td>
						<td>20</td>
						<td>Same as Sword Mastery but for Axes. Max this if you're going Axe.</td>
					</tr>
					<tr>
						<td><strong>Sword Booster</strong></td>
						<td>Buff</td>
						<td>20</td>
						<td>+2 attack speed while active. Consumes MP. Uptime maintenance.</td>
					</tr>
					<tr>
						<td><strong>Axe Booster</strong></td>
						<td>Buff</td>
						<td>20</td>
						<td>+2 attack speed for Axes.</td>
					</tr>
					<tr>
						<td><strong>Final Attack (Sword/Axe)</strong></td>
						<td>Passive proc</td>
						<td>30</td>
						<td>Chance to trigger a bonus swing after a basic attack. Damage% scales with skill level. One of the biggest DPS multipliers in the kit.</td>
					</tr>
					<tr>
						<td><strong>Rage</strong></td>
						<td>Party buff</td>
						<td>20</td>
						<td>+Weapon Attack to party for a duration. Best 2nd-job attacker buff in the game. Every party wants it.</td>
					</tr>
					<tr>
						<td><strong>Power Guard</strong></td>
						<td>Buff</td>
						<td>30</td>
						<td>Reflects a % of physical damage taken back at the attacker. Deprioritized until utility maxing.</td>
					</tr>
				</tbody>
			</table>
		</div>

		<h3>Fighter recommended build order</h3>
		<ol>
			<li><strong>Levels 30-35</strong>: 1 point in Sword Booster (or Axe Booster) - immediate DPS uptime. Then dump into your chosen Mastery.</li>
			<li><strong>Levels 35-45</strong>: Max your Mastery (20 points). Max your Booster next (~5-8 points is fine for 2 attack speed at low level; full 20 for uptime).</li>
			<li><strong>Levels 45-55</strong>: Max Final Attack (30 points). This is where your DPS actually spikes - proc chance + damage% both scale.</li>
			<li><strong>Levels 55-65</strong>: Max Rage (20 points). Now you're a party attacker AND a party buffer.</li>
			<li><strong>Levels 65-70</strong>: Power Guard filler until 70. Not a priority; feel free to save points for 3rd job if you'd rather.</li>
		</ol>

		<hr />

		<h2 id="page">Branch 2: Page</h2>
		<p class="section-lede">
			The Sword-or-Blunt-Weapon branch. Same weapon options as Fighter
			for Sword, but swaps Axe for BW (Blunt Weapon = maces, wands
			with attack). Lower damage than Fighter; makes up for it with
			debuff + reflection utility.
		</p>

		<h3>Weapon choice: Sword vs Blunt Weapon</h3>
		<p>
			Same speed profile as Fighter's Sword choice. BW is the
			replacement for Axe: slower base speed, higher base damage,
			same 1-handed/2-handed tradeoff. BW mastery caps slightly
			differently in v83 (both around 60% mastery ceiling at Lv 20).
		</p>
		<p>
			<strong>Sword is still the safe pick for Page.</strong> BW
			shines on specific weapons like the Deputy Star (1H) or
			Fusion Mace / Tobi (2H). If you're not chasing a specific
			end-of-2nd-job weapon, go Sword.
		</p>

		<h3>Page skill kit (v83 baseline, 8 skills)</h3>
		<div class="skill-list-wrap">
			<table class="skill-list">
				<thead>
					<tr>
						<th>Skill</th>
						<th>Type</th>
						<th>Max Lv</th>
						<th>What it does</th>
					</tr>
				</thead>
				<tbody>
					<tr>
						<td><strong>Sword Mastery</strong></td>
						<td>Passive</td>
						<td>20</td>
						<td>Same as Fighter's version. Max if Sword.</td>
					</tr>
					<tr>
						<td><strong>BW Mastery</strong></td>
						<td>Passive</td>
						<td>20</td>
						<td>Blunt Weapon mastery + accuracy. Max if BW.</td>
					</tr>
					<tr>
						<td><strong>Sword Booster</strong></td>
						<td>Buff</td>
						<td>20</td>
						<td>+2 attack speed for Swords.</td>
					</tr>
					<tr>
						<td><strong>BW Booster</strong></td>
						<td>Buff</td>
						<td>20</td>
						<td>+2 attack speed for Blunt Weapons.</td>
					</tr>
					<tr>
						<td><strong>Final Attack (Sword/BW)</strong></td>
						<td>Passive proc</td>
						<td>30</td>
						<td>Same bonus-swing mechanic as Fighter's Final Attack. Core DPS skill.</td>
					</tr>
					<tr>
						<td><strong>Threaten</strong></td>
						<td>Debuff</td>
						<td>20</td>
						<td>Lowers Weapon Attack + Weapon Defense of nearby enemies for a duration. Boss-fight utility. Signature Page skill.</td>
					</tr>
					<tr>
						<td><strong>Power Guard</strong></td>
						<td>Buff</td>
						<td>30</td>
						<td>Same as Fighter's version. On Page it's slightly more valuable because Page usually tanks in party comps.</td>
					</tr>
				</tbody>
			</table>
		</div>

		<h3>Page recommended build order</h3>
		<ol>
			<li><strong>Levels 30-35</strong>: 1 point in Booster. Dump into your Mastery.</li>
			<li><strong>Levels 35-45</strong>: Max Mastery (20 points). Max Booster (20 points) if you want full uptime, or ~5-8 as filler.</li>
			<li><strong>Levels 45-55</strong>: Max Final Attack (30 points). Same DPS reason as Fighter.</li>
			<li><strong>Levels 55-60</strong>: Max Threaten (20 points). This is your signature utility - use it on every boss.</li>
			<li><strong>Levels 60-70</strong>: Max Power Guard (30 points). Now you're the party's damage sponge. Note: Power Guard is more useful on Page than on Fighter/Spearman because Page routinely tanks Bosses.</li>
		</ol>

		<hr />

		<h2 id="spearman">Branch 3: Spearman</h2>
		<p class="section-lede">
			The Spear-or-Polearm branch. Party-support identity. Lower solo
			damage than Fighter, but Hyper Body at Lv 45 turns you into
			the most-invited attacker in the game.
		</p>

		<h3>Weapon choice: Spear vs Polearm</h3>
		<p>
			The Spearman weapon choice is the most consequential in the
			Warrior tree because it changes your <strong>attack animation
			pattern</strong>:
		</p>
		<ul>
			<li><strong>Spear</strong>: single-target thrust. Higher
				per-hit damage. Better for boss fights where you're
				facing one target.</li>
			<li><strong>Polearm</strong>: horizontal AoE swing that
				can hit 2+ mobs at once with basic attacks. Better for
				grinding. Slightly lower per-hit damage than Spear.</li>
		</ul>
		<p>
			<strong>Polearm is the standard pick.</strong> The AoE swing
			just outpaces Spear at every grinding spot for the whole
			2nd-job window. Save Spear for niche builds or boss-only
			specialists.
		</p>

		<h3>Spearman skill kit (v83 baseline, 8 skills)</h3>
		<div class="skill-list-wrap">
			<table class="skill-list">
				<thead>
					<tr>
						<th>Skill</th>
						<th>Type</th>
						<th>Max Lv</th>
						<th>What it does</th>
					</tr>
				</thead>
				<tbody>
					<tr>
						<td><strong>Spear Mastery</strong></td>
						<td>Passive</td>
						<td>20</td>
						<td>Spear mastery % + accuracy.</td>
					</tr>
					<tr>
						<td><strong>Polearm Mastery</strong></td>
						<td>Passive</td>
						<td>20</td>
						<td>Polearm mastery % + accuracy.</td>
					</tr>
					<tr>
						<td><strong>Spear Booster</strong></td>
						<td>Buff</td>
						<td>20</td>
						<td>+2 attack speed for Spears.</td>
					</tr>
					<tr>
						<td><strong>Polearm Booster</strong></td>
						<td>Buff</td>
						<td>20</td>
						<td>+2 attack speed for Polearms.</td>
					</tr>
					<tr>
						<td><strong>Final Attack (Spear/Polearm)</strong></td>
						<td>Passive proc</td>
						<td>30</td>
						<td>Same bonus-swing mechanic. Core DPS.</td>
					</tr>
					<tr>
						<td><strong>Iron Wall</strong></td>
						<td>Party buff</td>
						<td>20</td>
						<td>+Weapon Def + Magic Def to party. Support buff, priority scales with party density.</td>
					</tr>
					<tr>
						<td><strong>Hyper Body</strong></td>
						<td>Party buff</td>
						<td>30</td>
						<td>Multiplies party members' Max HP and Max MP for a duration. THE Spearman skill. Every party wants it. Every boss run needs it.</td>
					</tr>
				</tbody>
			</table>
		</div>

		<h3>Spearman recommended build order</h3>
		<ol>
			<li><strong>Levels 30-35</strong>: 1 point in Booster. Dump Mastery.</li>
			<li><strong>Levels 35-45</strong>: Max Mastery (20), then rush toward Hyper Body prereqs. Booster to ~5-8 for uptime.</li>
			<li><strong>Level 45</strong>: <strong>Immediately start Hyper Body</strong>. This is where party invites open up. Even 1 point is life-changing for your party.</li>
			<li><strong>Levels 45-55</strong>: Max Hyper Body (30 points). Nothing else in the game gives you this much party value at this level.</li>
			<li><strong>Levels 55-65</strong>: Max Final Attack (30 points). Now your damage catches up.</li>
			<li><strong>Levels 65-70</strong>: Max Iron Wall (20 points). Filler with real party value.</li>
		</ol>
		<p>
			<strong>Note on Hyper Body:</strong> This is the single most
			valuable 2nd-job party buff in the game. If you're playing
			solo, Fighter/Page might feel stronger - but the moment you
			start partying, you become irreplaceable. Nobody kicks the
			Hyper Body user.
		</p>

		<hr />

		<h2 id="progression">Common 2nd-job progression (30 to 70)</h2>

		<h3>Where to grind</h3>
		<ul>
			<li><strong>Lv 30-40</strong>: Ligators + Cursed Eyes at Kerning
				Swamp. Zombie Mushrooms + Wild Boars at Sleepywood Ant Tunnels.
				Party quests: Kerning PQ (21-30) tail end, then Ludi PQ (35+).</li>
			<li><strong>Lv 40-55</strong>: Copper Drakes + Drakes at
				Sleepywood. Wraiths at Kerning subway. Ludi PQ full window.</li>
			<li><strong>Lv 55-70</strong>: Fire Boars at Burning Land. Death
				Teddies at Ludi. Ludi Maze PQ (55-70). Orbis PQ if it ships.</li>
		</ul>

		<h3>Gear buys at Lv 30</h3>
		<p>
			The single biggest damage jump for a fresh Warrior 2nd-job is
			buying a <strong>level-appropriate weapon</strong>. Sword users
			target the Zamadar (Lv 30 Sword) or Doombringer (Lv 43 Sword).
			Axe users target Fusion Axe (Lv 40) or Steel Battle Axe (Lv 32).
			Polearm users target Fusion Halberd (Lv 40) or the classic
			Sky Ski (Lv 43). Prices fluctuate; always check the FM before
			committing.
		</p>
		<p>
			Armor priority: <strong>overall (armor covering top + bottom)</strong>
			over separates for the level bracket, then scrolls (10% white
			scrolls are the cheap sweet spot for 2nd job gear). Cape and
			shoulder are luxury; skip until Lv 50+.
		</p>

		<h3>What to expect at 3rd job (70)</h3>
		<ul>
			<li><strong>Fighter -&gt; Crusader</strong>: Combo Attack
				stacking mechanic, big AoE Panic/Coma finishers.</li>
			<li><strong>Page -&gt; White Knight</strong>: Elemental
				charge attacks (Fire/Ice/Lightning) that inflict status
				effects. Highly technical, extremely rewarding.</li>
			<li><strong>Spearman -&gt; Dragon Knight</strong>: Dragon
				Roar (huge AoE at HP cost), Sacrifice (self-buff
				damage bomb). Hyper Body still your calling card.</li>
		</ul>

		<h2 id="related">Related reading</h2>
		<ul>
			<li><a href="/jobs/warrior">Warrior 1st Job Guide</a> - if you're not there yet</li>
			<li><a href="/guides/skill-changes">Skill Changes: v83 vs CoT 2</a> - Warrior gained Precise Strikes at 1st job</li>
